"43_gamepane.mst — GamePane: drive the native Metal game pane from Smalltalk
 (docs/gamepane_design.md). Its methods speak numbered game primitives (the
 id-200+ block in src/runtime/primitives.rs) that emit GameCommands over the
 VM->GUI game channel; the GUI applies each to the native CAMetalLayer pane
 (gui/src/game_pane.rs). Drawing methods mutate the pane's buffer only.
 **Inside the frame loop (onStep:), end every frame with `present`** — that is
 the one moment the GUI shows the frame, and drawing without it flickers or
 freezes (the GUI deliberately does NOT auto-present mid-loop, so a half-drawn
 frame is never shown). For a one-shot draw OUTSIDE the loop (a Workspace doit),
 `present` is optional — the response drain shows anything dirty.

 Colour arguments are 0..255; palette index 0 is transparent, and user palette
 entries start at 16. In a headless VM (no game sink) every primitive silently
 fails and returns self, so this class is harmless to load outside the GUI."

"--- Sprite: a handle to a placed sprite instance (docs/gamepane_design.md).
 Defined before GamePane so GamePane>>defineSprite: can reference it. ---"
"I am a handle to one sprite instance placed on a GamePane. Obtain me from
 GamePane>>defineSprite: (which defines the pixel art and places me). Set
 my palette entries with colorAt:r:g:b: (index 0..15, channels 0..255) and
 move me with moveTo:y:; changes show on the next presented frame."
Object subclass: Sprite [
    | pane id |
    Sprite class >> pane: aPane id: anId [
        ^self new setPane: aPane id: anId
    ]
    setPane: aPane id: anId [ pane := aPane. id := anId. ^self ]
    moveTo: x y: y [
        "Move this sprite to (x, y)."
        pane primMoveSprite: id x: x y: y.
        ^self
    ]
    colorAt: index r: r g: g b: b [
        "Set this sprite's palette entry `index` (0..15) to an RGB colour."
        pane primSpriteColor: id index: index r: r g: g b: b.
        ^self
    ]
    addFrame: hexRows [
        "Append another frame of art to my definition (same size + palette as
         the first) — makes me a multi-frame sprite. Answer myself so frames
         chain onto a fresh sprite. Select the frame later with moveTo:y:frame:."
        pane primAddFrame: id rows: hexRows.
        ^self
    ]
    moveTo: x y: y frame: f [
        "Move me to (x, y) AND show frame `f` (0-based) of my definition."
        pane primPlace: id x: x y: y frame: f.
        ^self
    ]
    hide [
        "Stop drawing me until my next moveTo:."
        pane primHide: id.
        ^self
    ]
]

"--- GameFrame: the parameters one frame hands the game. Defined before
 GamePane so GamePane can reference it (name resolution is compile-time,
 per file). ---"
"I am the bundle of parameters the engine hands a game once per frame:
 which keys are held, where the pointer is and which buttons are down. A
 step block can take me as its argument — `onStep: [ :frame | ... frame
 mouseX ... ]` — or ignore me and ask the pane instead (`pane mouseX`),
 which forwards to whichever frame is current. I exist so that giving games
 a NEW per-frame parameter means adding an instance variable here, not
 another class variable on GamePane: the pane's class-side shell is merged
 into the image on every reseed, and growing it once already cost a
 world-boot regression (docs/gamepane_design.md)."
Object subclass: GameFrame [
    | keys mouseX mouseY mouseButtons |

    GameFrame class >> new [ ^super new initFrame ]

    initFrame [
        "A frame nobody has filled in yet: no keys held, and a pointer that has
         never been over the pane (-1, the same 'nowhere' the GUI sends before
         the mouse arrives)."
        keys := 0. mouseX := -1. mouseY := -1. mouseButtons := 0.
        ^self
    ]

    keys: k [
        "Record just the held-key mask — the shape the WKWebView GUI sends,
         which has no mouse to report. Leaves the pointer as it was."
        keys := k.
        ^self
    ]

    keys: k mouseX: mx y: my buttons: mb [
        "Record a whole frame's input — what the Cocoa GUI's frame timer sends."
        keys := k. mouseX := mx. mouseY := my. mouseButtons := mb.
        ^self
    ]

    keyHeld: keyCode [
        "Is game key `keyCode` (GamePane class>>keyLeft etc.) held this frame?"
        ^(keys bitAnd: (1 bitShift: keyCode)) ~= 0
    ]

    "The pointer, in PANE PIXELS from the top-left — the same coordinates
     point:y:color: and blit: use, converted by the GUI from the window's own
     size so they stay right when the game window is resized. Buttons are
     LEVEL-triggered (held, not clicked): a demo wanting 'the moment it went
     down' compares against what it saw last frame, exactly as keyHeld: asks."
    mouseX [ "Pointer x in pane pixels (-1 before it enters)." ^mouseX ]
    mouseY [ "Pointer y in pane pixels (-1 before it enters)." ^mouseY ]
    mouseButtons [ "Raw button bitmask: 1 left, 2 right." ^mouseButtons ]
    mouseDown [ "Is the left button held?" ^(mouseButtons bitAnd: 1) ~= 0 ]
    mouseRightDown [ "Is the right button held?" ^(mouseButtons bitAnd: 2) ~= 0 ]
    mouseInPane [ "Has the pointer been over the pane at all yet?" ^mouseX >= 0 ]
]

"I drive the native game pane from Smalltalk: a low-resolution palette
 display with drawing primitives, sprites, sound, keyboard input and a
 frame loop. Draw with cls:, point:y:color:, line:y:to:y:color:,
 fill:y:width:height:color:, disc:y:radius:color:, or blit: a whole frame
 of palette indices in one command; set colours with paletteAt:r:g:b:
 (user entries 16..255, channels 0..255; index 0 is transparent). Define
 sprites with defineSprite: (answers a Sprite handle). Register a
 per-frame block with onStep: — and end every frame with `present`, the
 one moment the GUI shows it — plus optional cleanup with onReset:; then
 `run` starts the GUI-owned frame timer and `stop` ends it. Inside the
 step block, poll held keys with keyHeld: and my class-side key constants
 (keyLeft, keyRight, keyUp, keyDown, keyA, keyB), and the pointer with
 mouseX/mouseY (pane pixels, top-left origin) plus mouseDown/mouseRightDown.
 In a headless VM every game primitive silently fails, so I am harmless to
 load outside the GUI. Breakout, MandelZoom and Life are complete worked
 examples."
Object subclass: GamePane [
    "One slot for the whole per-frame parameter set (a GameFrame), not one per
     parameter: every class variable here has to survive the image's
     shell-merge on reseed, so the list should stop growing as the engine
     learns new inputs."
    <classVars: StepBlock NextId ResetBlock Frame>

    "── palettes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A fresh pane arrives with COLOURS ALREADY IN IT. Before this, a new
     pane had an empty user palette, so the first thing anyone drew came
     out invisible — `color: 1` (the obvious guess) is below the user range
     and silently draws nothing, and even `color: 16` was unset until you
     said `paletteAt:`. A black window with no error is the least friendly
     thing this engine did, and it was the first thing a beginner met.
     Now slots 16..31 hold a general-purpose 16-colour set from the moment
     you say `GamePane new`; override any entry with `paletteAt:r:g:b:` as
     before, or swap the whole set with `palette:` below."

    GamePane class >> new [
        ^super new installPalette: #default
    ]

    installPalette: aSymbol [
        "Load a named 16-colour set into slots 16 upward. Answers self so
         `GamePane new` can chain it."
        | rgbs i |
        rgbs := GamePane paletteNamed: aSymbol.
        i := 16.
        rgbs do: [ :c |
            self paletteAt: i r: (c at: 1) g: (c at: 2) b: (c at: 3).
            i := i + 1 ].
        ^self
    ]

    palette: aSymbol [
        "Swap the whole palette for a named set — `pane palette: #c64`.
         See `GamePane paletteNames`."
        ^self installPalette: aSymbol
    ]

    GamePane class >> paletteNames [
        ^#(#default #cga #c64 #spectrum #msx #gameboy #mono)
    ]

    GamePane class >> rgb: r g: g b: b [ ^Array with: r with: g with: b ]

    GamePane class >> paletteNamed: aSymbol [
        "The colour sets, each an Array of (r g b) triples loaded from slot
         16 up. The retro sets are the real hardware palettes — a C64 game
         looks like a C64 game because these are the only colours it had."
        aSymbol == #cga ifTrue: [ ^self cgaPalette ].
        aSymbol == #c64 ifTrue: [ ^self c64Palette ].
        aSymbol == #spectrum ifTrue: [ ^self spectrumPalette ].
        aSymbol == #msx ifTrue: [ ^self msxPalette ].
        aSymbol == #gameboy ifTrue: [ ^self gameboyPalette ].
        aSymbol == #mono ifTrue: [ ^self monoPalette ].
        ^self defaultPalette
    ]

    "A modern, friendly 16 — dark tones first, then the brights, so a
     gradient of indices reads as a gradient of colour."
    GamePane class >> defaultPalette [
        | p |
        p := OrderedCollection new.
        p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 0   b: 0).      "16 black"
        p add: (self rgb: 29  g: 43  b: 83).     "17 midnight"
        p add: (self rgb: 126 g: 37  b: 83).     "18 plum"
        p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 135 b: 81).     "19 forest"
        p add: (self rgb: 171 g: 82  b: 54).     "20 brown"
        p add: (self rgb: 95  g: 87  b: 79).     "21 dark grey"
        p add: (self rgb: 194 g: 195 b: 199).    "22 light grey"
        p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 241 b: 232).    "23 white"
        p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 0   b: 77).     "24 red"
        p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 163 b: 0).      "25 orange"
        p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 236 b: 39).     "26 yellow"
        p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 228 b: 54).     "27 green"
        p add: (self rgb: 41  g: 173 b: 255).    "28 blue"
        p add: (self rgb: 131 g: 118 b: 156).    "29 lavender"
        p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 119 b: 168).    "30 pink"
        p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 204 b: 170).    "31 peach"
        ^p asArray
    ]

    "IBM CGA/EGA — the 16 colours of early PC games."
    GamePane class >> cgaPalette [
        | p |
        p := OrderedCollection new.
        p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 0   b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 0   b: 170).
        p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 170 b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 170 b: 170).
        p add: (self rgb: 170 g: 0   b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 170 g: 0   b: 170).
        p add: (self rgb: 170 g: 85  b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 170 g: 170 b: 170).
        p add: (self rgb: 85  g: 85  b: 85).   p add: (self rgb: 85  g: 85  b: 255).
        p add: (self rgb: 85  g: 255 b: 85).   p add: (self rgb: 85  g: 255 b: 255).
        p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 85  b: 85).   p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 85  b: 255).
        p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 255 b: 85).   p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 255 b: 255).
        ^p asArray
    ]

    "Commodore 64 — VIC-II."
    GamePane class >> c64Palette [
        | p |
        p := OrderedCollection new.
        p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 0   b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 255 b: 255).
        p add: (self rgb: 136 g: 57  b: 50).   p add: (self rgb: 103 g: 182 b: 189).
        p add: (self rgb: 139 g: 63  b: 150).  p add: (self rgb: 85  g: 160 b: 73).
        p add: (self rgb: 64  g: 49  b: 141).  p add: (self rgb: 191 g: 206 b: 114).
        p add: (self rgb: 139 g: 84  b: 41).   p add: (self rgb: 87  g: 66  b: 0).
        p add: (self rgb: 184 g: 105 b: 98).   p add: (self rgb: 80  g: 80  b: 80).
        p add: (self rgb: 120 g: 120 b: 120).  p add: (self rgb: 148 g: 224 b: 137).
        p add: (self rgb: 120 g: 105 b: 196).  p add: (self rgb: 159 g: 159 b: 159).
        ^p asArray
    ]

    "ZX Spectrum — eight colours, twice, normal then bright."
    GamePane class >> spectrumPalette [
        | p |
        p := OrderedCollection new.
        p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 0   b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 0   b: 215).
        p add: (self rgb: 215 g: 0   b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 215 g: 0   b: 215).
        p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 215 b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 215 b: 215).
        p add: (self rgb: 215 g: 215 b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 215 g: 215 b: 215).
        p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 0   b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 0   b: 255).
        p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 0   b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 0   b: 255).
        p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 255 b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 255 b: 255).
        p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 255 b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 255 b: 255).
        ^p asArray
    ]

    "MSX / TMS9918 — the colours of a generation of Japanese home computers."
    GamePane class >> msxPalette [
        | p |
        p := OrderedCollection new.
        p add: (self rgb: 0   g: 0   b: 0).    p add: (self rgb: 33  g: 200 b: 66).
        p add: (self rgb: 94  g: 220 b: 120).  p add: (self rgb: 84  g: 85  b: 237).
        p add: (self rgb: 125 g: 118 b: 252).  p add: (self rgb: 212 g: 82  b: 77).
        p add: (self rgb: 66  g: 235 b: 245).  p add: (self rgb: 252 g: 85  b: 84).
        p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 121 b: 120).  p add: (self rgb: 212 g: 193 b: 84).
        p add: (self rgb: 230 g: 206 b: 128).  p add: (self rgb: 33  g: 176 b: 59).
        p add: (self rgb: 201 g: 91  b: 186).  p add: (self rgb: 204 g: 204 b: 204).
        p add: (self rgb: 255 g: 255 b: 255).  p add: (self rgb: 128 g: 128 b: 128).
        ^p asArray
    ]

    "Game Boy — four shades of green, repeated so any index reads."
    GamePane class >> gameboyPalette [
        | p |
        p := OrderedCollection new.
        1 to: 4 do: [ :i |
            p add: (self rgb: 15  g: 56  b: 15).
            p add: (self rgb: 48  g: 98  b: 48).
            p add: (self rgb: 139 g: 172 b: 15).
            p add: (self rgb: 155 g: 188 b: 15) ].
        ^p asArray
    ]

    "Sixteen greys, black to white — good for shading experiments."
    GamePane class >> monoPalette [
        | p v |
        p := OrderedCollection new.
        0 to: 15 do: [ :i | v := i * 17. p add: (self rgb: v g: v b: v) ].
        ^p asArray
    ]

    "Names for the default palette's slots, so a beginner can write
     `color: GamePane red` instead of remembering a number. These are
     indices into the DEFAULT set; after `palette: #c64` the numbers still
     work but the colours are the C64's."
    GamePane class >> black     [ ^16 ]
    GamePane class >> midnight  [ ^17 ]
    GamePane class >> plum      [ ^18 ]
    GamePane class >> forest    [ ^19 ]
    GamePane class >> brown     [ ^20 ]
    GamePane class >> darkGrey  [ ^21 ]
    GamePane class >> lightGrey [ ^22 ]
    GamePane class >> white     [ ^23 ]
    GamePane class >> red       [ ^24 ]
    GamePane class >> orange    [ ^25 ]
    GamePane class >> yellow    [ ^26 ]
    GamePane class >> green     [ ^27 ]
    GamePane class >> blue      [ ^28 ]
    GamePane class >> lavender  [ ^29 ]
    GamePane class >> pink      [ ^30 ]
    GamePane class >> peach     [ ^31 ]

    clearR: r g: g b: b [
        "Clear the pane to an opaque RGB colour (each 0..255) and present it."
        <primitive: 200>
        ^self
    ]
    paletteAt: index r: r g: g b: b [
        "Set palette entry `index` (16..255) to an opaque RGB colour."
        <primitive: 201>
        ^self
    ]
    cls: index [
        "Clear the pane to palette `index` (0..255)."
        <primitive: 202>
        ^self
    ]
    point: x y: y color: c [
        "Plot a pixel at (x, y) in palette colour `c`."
        <primitive: 203>
        ^self
    ]
    line: x0 y: y0 to: x1 y: y1 color: c [
        "Draw a line from (x0, y0) to (x1, y1) in palette colour `c`."
        <primitive: 204>
        ^self
    ]
    fill: x y: y width: w height: h color: c [
        "Fill a w x h rectangle at (x, y) in palette colour `c`."
        <primitive: 205>
        ^self
    ]
    disc: cx y: cy radius: r color: c [
        "Fill a disc of radius `r` centred at (cx, cy) in palette colour `c`."
        <primitive: 206>
        ^self
    ]
    present [
        "Show the frame drawn so far."
        <primitive: 207>
        ^self
    ]
    blit: aByteArray [
        "Overwrite the whole pane from a ByteArray of width*height palette
         indices (row-major, 0 at top-left) in ONE command — the bulk path for a
         CPU-generated frame, far cheaper than a point:y:color: per pixel.
         Follow it with `present` to show the frame (required inside the loop)."
        <primitive: 215>
        ^self
    ]

    "--- the frame loop (docs/gamepane_design.md M4) ---"

    onStep: aBlock [
        "Register the per-frame block. It is held in a class variable, so it is
         GC-rooted for the whole game (and so is everything it closes over).

         Take an argument to be handed this frame's parameters — the keys and
         the pointer, as a GameFrame:

             pane onStep: [ :frame |
                 frame mouseDown ifTrue: [ ... frame mouseX ... ] ]

         or take none and reach the same values through the pane
         (`pane mouseDown`, `pane keyHeld:`), which is what the older demos
         do. Both are supported; the argument form is the one that keeps
         working unchanged as the engine gains new per-frame inputs."
        StepBlock := aBlock.
        ^self
    ]
    onReset: aBlock [
        "Register a one-shot cleanup block, run by `reset` (below) the moment
         this session ends — however it ends: Escape, the close button, a
         re-launch while this demo is still running, or an orphaned pane
         force-closed by a primary-VM restart. For a demo with per-session
         native/worker state beyond StepBlock (ParallelMandel's spawned
         workers), THIS is the clean-teardown hook — without it, that state
         has no reset path at all and just accumulates across runs."
        ResetBlock := aBlock.
        ^self
    ]
    run [
        "Start the frame loop: the GUI's main-thread timer begins pulling one
         frame per tick. Returns immediately — the loop is owned by the timer,
         not by a blocking Smalltalk loop."
        <primitive: 208>
        ^self
    ]
    stop [
        "Stop the frame loop."
        <primitive: 209>
        ^self
    ]
    keyHeld: keyCode [
        "Is game key `keyCode` (see GamePane class>>keyLeft etc.) held this
         frame? Only meaningful inside the step block."
        ^GamePane keyHeld: keyCode
    ]

    "--- the mouse, from inside a step block that took no argument ---
     `pane mouseDown ifTrue: [ ... pane mouseX ... pane mouseY ... ]`. Each of
     these forwards to the current GameFrame, where the real documentation
     lives; a block written `[ :frame | ... ]` asks that object directly
     instead, and is the form that survives the engine gaining new inputs."

    mouseX [ "Pointer x in pane pixels this frame (-1 before it enters)." ^GamePane mouseX ]
    mouseY [ "Pointer y in pane pixels this frame (-1 before it enters)." ^GamePane mouseY ]
    mouseDown [ "Is the left button held this frame?" ^GamePane mouseDown ]
    mouseRightDown [ "Is the right button held this frame?" ^GamePane mouseRightDown ]
    mouseInPane [ "Has the pointer been over the pane yet?" ^GamePane mouseInPane ]

    "--- sprites (docs/gamepane_design.md) ---"

    defineSprite: hexRows [
        "Define a sprite from '/'-separated hex-row art (4 bits/pixel, so up to
         16 colours per sprite; e.g. 'f0f/0f0/f0f' is 3x3) and place it; answer
         a Sprite handle. Set its colours with colorAt:r:g:b: and move it with
         moveTo:y:."
        | id |
        id := GamePane nextId.
        self primDefineSprite: id rows: hexRows.
        ^Sprite pane: self id: id
    ]
    primDefineSprite: id rows: hexRows [ <primitive: 210> ^self ]
    primSpriteColor: id index: index r: r g: g b: b [ <primitive: 211> ^self ]
    primMoveSprite: id x: x y: y [ <primitive: 212> ^self ]

    "--- GamePane extensions: features the MacGamePane engine already renders
     (docs/gamepane_design.md). All harmless in a headless VM (the primitive
     fails and the `^self` fallback runs). ---"

    resizeTo: w by: h [
        "Recreate the pane (indexed buffer + text overlay + shader layer) at
         `w`x`h`. Call BEFORE drawing if the default 320x240 doesn't fit; a
         demo that never calls this keeps 320x240. galaxigans is 640x360."
        <primitive: 253>
        ^self
    ]

    "--- the palette, as memory (docs/DIRECT_SCREEN.md SM4) ---
     The colour table itself, as RGBA bytes, four per entry - so a demo can
     rewrite colours with stores instead of a command each. That matters most
     for the PER-SCANLINE entries: a copper effect rewrites what a colour
     means on every scanline every frame, which is 240 commands a frame
     through linePaletteAt: and none at all through here.

     Layout: `height` groups of 16 per-line entries first, so line `y` colour
     `i` (1..15) is entry `y * 16 + i`; then the 240 global entries, so colour
     `c` (16..255) is entry `paletteGlobalBase + c - 16`. Within an entry the
     bytes are red, green, blue, alpha. Leave alpha at 255.

     paletteAt:r:g:b: and linePaletteAt:... still work and write the very same
     buffer - there is one palette and no copy of it anywhere, which is what
     stops a per-frame upload ever clobbering what a demo wrote."

    paletteMemory [
        "The palette as an Alien of RGBA bytes; nil with no pane open."
        <primitive: 275>
        ^nil
    ]
    paletteGlobalBase [
        "The ENTRY index where the 240 global colours begin. Ask rather than
         assume: it is the viewport height times sixteen."
        <primitive: 276>
        ^nil
    ]

    paletteMemoryAt: entry put: rgb [
        "Write one palette ENTRY from a packed 0xRRGGBB, straight into the
         buffer. The unit is an ENTRY, not a byte, because every caller thinks
         in entries and the times-four is the easy thing to get wrong."
        | pm base |
        pm := self paletteMemory.
        pm isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        base := entry * 4.
        pm byteAt: base + 1 put: ((rgb bitShift: -16) bitAnd: 255).
        pm byteAt: base + 2 put: ((rgb bitShift: -8) bitAnd: 255).
        pm byteAt: base + 3 put: (rgb bitAnd: 255).
        pm byteAt: base + 4 put: 255.
        ^self
    ]

    lineMemoryAt: line index: i put: rgb [
        "The per-scanline entry for colour `i` on scanline `line`, written
         directly. The copper case, and the reason this exists."
        ^self paletteMemoryAt: (line * 16) + i put: rgb
    ]

    "--- direct screen memory (docs/shared_screen_memory_design.md) ---
     The fast path for a demo that computes every pixel: instead of filling a
     ByteArray and shipping it here with blit:, ask me for the framebuffer
     ITSELF and write into it. On Apple Silicon the memory the GPU samples is
     ordinary memory, so `screenMemory` hands back an Alien over it and a
     store into that Alien IS a store into the screen. No command, no copy.

         pane openDirect: 320 height: 240.
         fb := pane screenMemory.        'refetch each frame — see below'
         s  := pane screenStride.
         fb byteAt: (y * s) + x + 1 put: colourIndex.
         pane present.

     TWO THINGS TO GET RIGHT. The stride is NOT the width — a row is
     `screenStride` bytes wide because the GPU rounds it up, so address
     `fb byteAt: y * stride + x + 1` and never `y * width + x`. And refetch
     `screenMemory` every frame: presenting rotates which buffer you are
     writing (that rotation is what stops you drawing into the one the GPU is
     reading), so a kept Alien goes stale."

    openDirect: w height: h [
        "Build a direct framebuffer `w` by `h` in GPU-shared memory. Send this
         before asking for screenMemory. Colours are palette indices exactly as
         everywhere else, so paletteAt:r:g:b: still applies."
        <primitive: 268>
        ^self
    ]

    screenMemory [
        "The framebuffer as an Alien, or nil if no direct pane is open (a
         headless VM always answers nil, so a demo can check and fall back to
         blit:). The Alien is LENGTH-BOUNDED: writing past the end fails
         rather than corrupting whatever follows the screen."
        <primitive: 269>
        ^nil
    ]

    screenStride [
        "Bytes per row of the direct framebuffer — see the warning above. nil
         if no direct pane is open."
        <primitive: 270>
        ^nil
    ]

    "--- the text plane (docs/shared_screen_memory_design.md SM1) ---
     The text overlay, seen as a SCREEN BUFFER instead of a chain of commands.
     A grid of cells in shared memory: four bytes each — character, foreground
     colour, background colour, flags — that the GPU turns into glyphs. Writing
     a character is a STORE, so a HUD redrawn every frame costs nothing, and
     there is no rule to remember about only redrawing when something changed.
     That rule existed because `text:x:y:...` sends a command per string; this
     sends none at all.

         pane textPut: 'SCORE 100' at: 0 row: 0 color: GamePane white.
         pane textClearPlane.

     Character 0 means an UNUSED cell and draws nothing — not a black square —
     so the plane is invisible until you write into it, and clearing a cell
     really does remove it.

     Cells are 6x8 pixels (the 5x7 font plus a pixel of leading), so a 320x240
     pane is 53x30 cells. That grid is the limit as well as the point: glyphs
     land on cell boundaries, so this is for text SCREENS — HUDs, menus, help
     pages, consoles. Text that must sit at an arbitrary pixel offset (a digit
     centred in a 16-pixel square, a card's rank inside its corner) stays pixel
     art in the picture itself."

    textMemory [
        "The cell grid as an Alien — `textCols * textRows` cells of 4 bytes,
         row by row. nil when no pane is open. Unlike screenMemory this never
         moves, so it is safe to keep."
        <primitive: 271>
        ^nil
    ]
    textCols [ "Grid width in cells; nil with no pane." <primitive: 272> ^nil ]
    textRows [ "Grid height in cells; nil with no pane." <primitive: 273> ^nil ]

    textAt: col row: row put: aCharacter color: fg back: bg flags: fl [
        "Write one cell. Silently ignores anything off the grid, so a HUD that
         runs long clips instead of failing."
        | tm base |
        tm := self textMemory.
        tm isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        (col >= 0 and: [ row >= 0 and: [ col < self textCols and: [ row < self textRows ] ] ])
            ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        base := ((row * self textCols) + col) * 4.
        tm byteAt: base + 1 put: aCharacter asInteger.
        tm byteAt: base + 2 put: fg.
        tm byteAt: base + 3 put: bg.
        tm byteAt: base + 4 put: fl.
        ^self
    ]

    textPut: aString at: col row: row color: fg [
        "Write a string in `fg` over whatever is behind it (transparent
         background) — the common case, and the one a HUD wants."
        ^self textPut: aString at: col row: row color: fg back: 0 flags: 1
    ]

    textPut: aString at: col row: row color: fg back: bg flags: fl [
        "Write a string cell by cell. One store per character and not one
         command, however often it is called."
        | tm |
        tm := self textMemory.
        tm isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        1 to: aString size do: [ :i |
            self textAt: col + i - 1 row: row
                 put: (aString at: i) color: fg back: bg flags: fl ].
        ^self
    ]

    textBlast: aByteArray [
        "Show a whole prepared page AT ONCE — `cols * rows * 4` bytes of cells,
         copied into the text plane in a single memcpy rather than a store per
         character (SM3).

         This is what makes a menu, a help page or a console screen INSTANT:
         compose it once into a TextScreen, keep it, and put it up whenever you
         like for the cost of one copy. Nothing is redrawn and nothing is sent.

         A page of the wrong size is refused rather than half-applied."
        | tm n |
        tm := self textMemory.
        tm isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        n := self textCols * self textRows * 4.
        aByteArray size = n ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        tm replaceFrom: 1 to: n with: aByteArray startingAt: 1.
        ^self
    ]

    textClearPlane [
        "Blank every cell — the plane goes invisible again. Cheap: it is a
         memory wipe, not a redraw."
        | tm n |
        tm := self textMemory.
        tm isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        n := self textCols * self textRows * 4.
        1 to: n do: [ :i | tm byteAt: i put: 0 ].
        ^self
    ]

    overscan: margin [
        "Build me with a WORLD `margin` pixels bigger than my viewport on every
         side, so the view can be scrolled around inside it. Like resizeTo:by:
         this decides how I am BUILT — send it before drawing anything. The
         view starts centred, at scrollTo: margin y: margin, so it can move
         either way. Drawing coordinates are then WORLD coordinates: with a
         margin of 16 the top-left of the visible area is (16, 16).

         The point of the margin is scrollTo:y: below — pixels you have already
         drawn, sitting just off-screen, ready to be scrolled into view for
         free."
        <primitive: 266>
        ^self
    ]

    scrollTo: x y: y [
        "Move the viewport to (x, y) in the world. This does NOT redraw: it
         sets a shader uniform, so nothing is rasterized and nothing is
         re-uploaded — panning or shaking the view costs one command a frame
         rather than a frame's worth of pixels. Clamped to the world, so you
         cannot scroll off your own edge. Needs `overscan:` to have any room to
         move in (with no overscan the world IS the viewport and every scroll
         clamps to 0,0). Minesweeper's explosion shake is this and nothing
         else."
        <primitive: 267>
        ^self
    ]

    frameRate: fps [
        "Request the frame timer's rate in frames-per-second (1..120). The
         default is 60; a demo whose logic was tuned for a slower rate asks for
         it here (galaxigans uses 30). Resets to 60 when the window closes."
        <primitive: 262>
        ^self
    ]

    "The topmost text overlay: a real 5x7 ASCII font drawn in RGB (not palette
     indices), each font pixel blocked by `scale`. RETAINED between frames, so
     a changing HUD calls textClear first (else each frame prints over the
     last until the glyphs smear solid)."
    "The primitive takes a single packed 0xRRGGBB colour (an immediate
     SmallInteger — a zero-alloc 'structure') so it stays within the primitive
     argument limit; the r:g:b: forms below just pack it for callers."
    text: aString x: x y: y rgb: rgb scale: k [ <primitive: 254> ^self ]
    text: aString x: x y: y r: r g: g b: b scale: k [
        ^self text: aString x: x y: y rgb: ((r bitShift: 16) + (g bitShift: 8) + b) scale: k
    ]
    text: aString x: x y: y r: r g: g b: b [
        ^self text: aString x: x y: y r: r g: g b: b scale: 1
    ]
    textClear [ <primitive: 255> ^self ]

    "Multi-frame sprites: give a definition more frames, then place a specific
     one. Reached through Sprite>>addFrame:/moveTo:y:frame:/hide."
    primAddFrame: id rows: hexRows [ <primitive: 256> ^self ]
    primPlace: id x: x y: y frame: f [ <primitive: 257> ^self ]
    primHide: id [ <primitive: 258> ^self ]

    "The layer-0 background shader: compile a Metal fragment shader once, then
     drive its uniforms per frame. `shaderParam: 0 value: n` sets p[0]. The
     shader reads u.time, u.aspect, u.p[0..7] and in.uv, returns float4."
    shader: mslSource [ <primitive: 259> ^self ]
    shaderParam: index value: aNumber [ <primitive: 260> ^self ]

    "The per-scanline (copper-bar) palette: palette index `i` (1..15) can hold
     a different colour on every scanline, so a shape drawn ONCE in index `i`
     changes colour down the screen and animates by rewriting the line colours
     — no pixel redrawn (galaxigans' tractor beam)."
    linePaletteAt: line index: i rgb: rgb [ <primitive: 261> ^self ]
    linePaletteAt: line index: i r: r g: g b: b [
        ^self linePaletteAt: line index: i rgb: ((r bitShift: 16) + (g bitShift: 8) + b)
    ]

    GamePane class >> frame [
        "This frame's parameters — the single object the engine fills in each
         tick and hands to the game. Created on demand, so a headless VM that
         never steps still answers every query sensibly."
        Frame isNil ifTrue: [ Frame := GameFrame new ].
        ^Frame
    ]

    GamePane class >> stepWithKeys: mask [
        "Run one frame from a held-key mask alone — the WKWebView GUI's shape,
         and what the headless tests drive. Leaves the pointer as it was."
        self frame keys: mask.
        ^self runStep
    ]
    GamePane class >> stepWithKeys: mask mouseX: mx y: my buttons: mb [
        "Run one frame with the mouse as well as the keys — what the Cocoa
         GUI's frame timer sends. Filling the frame BEFORE running the block
         means the game sees this frame's input, not the last one's."
        self frame keys: mask mouseX: mx y: my buttons: mb.
        ^self runStep
    ]
    GamePane class >> runStep [
        "Run the registered step block, handing it this frame's parameters if
         it takes an argument. Both shapes are first-class: `onStep: [ ... ]`
         reads the frame through the pane (`pane mouseX`), while
         `onStep: [ :frame | ... ]` is given it directly — which is the form
         that stays honest as the engine learns new per-frame inputs, since
         they arrive as messages on the frame rather than new global state.
         Called once per tick on an idle worker (never nested)."
        StepBlock isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        StepBlock numArgs = 1
            ifTrue: [ StepBlock value: self frame ]
            ifFalse: [ StepBlock value ].
        ^self
    ]

    "--- input, class side: every one of these now forwards to the current
     frame, so `GamePane keyHeld:` keeps meaning exactly what it did for
     Breakout, Worms, Galaxigans and FftScope. ---"

    GamePane class >> mouseX [ ^self frame mouseX ]
    GamePane class >> mouseY [ ^self frame mouseY ]
    GamePane class >> mouseButtons [ ^self frame mouseButtons ]
    GamePane class >> mouseDown [ "Is the LEFT button held this frame?" ^self frame mouseDown ]
    GamePane class >> mouseRightDown [ "Is the RIGHT button held this frame?" ^self frame mouseRightDown ]
    GamePane class >> mouseInPane [ "Has the pointer been over the pane yet?" ^self frame mouseInPane ]
    GamePane class >> keyHeld: keyCode [
        "Bit `keyCode` of this frame's held-key mask."
        ^self frame keyHeld: keyCode
    ]
    GamePane class >> reset [
        "Run and clear whatever cleanup a demo registered via `onReset:` FIRST
         (while its StepBlock closure is still intact to poke at), then the
         frame-loop state itself. MACVM has no exception system (fail fast is
         the house rule) — a broken teardown block surfaces normally, exactly
         like any other guest error."
        ResetBlock ifNotNil: [ ResetBlock value. ResetBlock := nil ].
        StepBlock := nil.
        "Drop the frame entirely rather than zeroing fields one by one — the
         next session gets a fresh one, and this line does not need editing
         when the frame learns a new parameter."
        Frame := nil.
    ]
    GamePane class >> nextId [
        "A fresh monotonic sprite id. The GUI registry maps it to MacGamePane's
         own def/instance ids (docs/gamepane_design.md)."
        NextId isNil ifTrue: [ NextId := 0 ].
        NextId := NextId + 1.
        ^NextId
    ]

    "Game-key bit positions (the GUI packs these from the held-key table)."
    GamePane class >> keyLeft  [ ^0 ]
    GamePane class >> keyRight [ ^1 ]
    GamePane class >> keyUp    [ ^2 ]
    GamePane class >> keyDown  [ ^3 ]
    GamePane class >> keyA     [ ^4 ]
    GamePane class >> keyB     [ ^5 ]
]

"--- Sound: a named SFX preset played on the one shared engine
 (docs/gamepane_design.md). `Sound coin play`. ---"
"I am a named sound-effect preset played on the shared game audio engine:
 `Sound coin play`. My class-side constructors name the presets — coin,
 jump, zap, shoot, explode, powerup, hurt, click, bang, blip. In a
 headless VM `play` is silently a no-op."
Object subclass: Sound [
    | preset |
    Sound class >> preset: n [ ^self new setPreset: n ]
    Sound class >> coin    [ ^self preset: 0 ]
    Sound class >> jump    [ ^self preset: 1 ]
    Sound class >> zap     [ ^self preset: 2 ]
    Sound class >> shoot   [ ^self preset: 3 ]
    Sound class >> explode [ ^self preset: 4 ]
    Sound class >> powerup [ ^self preset: 5 ]
    Sound class >> hurt    [ ^self preset: 6 ]
    Sound class >> click   [ ^self preset: 7 ]
    Sound class >> bang    [ ^self preset: 8 ]
    Sound class >> blip    [ ^self preset: 9 ]
    "Beyond MACVM's original ten (galaxigans): a UFO warble (two sines a few
     Hz apart, the beat IS the wah) and the capture boss's tractor hum (the
     same beating trick an octave-and-a-half down and slower)."
    Sound class >> saucer  [ ^self preset: 10 ]
    Sound class >> bossHum [ ^self preset: 11 ]
    setPreset: n [ preset := n. ^self ]
    play [ "Play this sound." self primPlay: preset. ^self ]
    primPlay: n [ <primitive: 213> ^self ]

    "The parametric wire (docs/asset_editors_design.md §3): render a full
     Effect recipe — the flat params contract — and play it. What the Sound
     Editor auditions through, and what a saved sound sheet's `play` calls.
     Headless: silently a no-op, like every game primitive."
    Sound class >> playEffect: paramsArray [ <primitive: 263> ^self ]
]

"--- Tune: an ABC-notation chiptune, played once in the background
 (docs/gamepane_design.md). `(Tune fromAbc: 'C C G G A A G2') playOnce`. ---"
"I am a chiptune written in ABC notation, played in the background by the
 game audio engine: (Tune fromAbc: 'C C G G A A G2') playOnce. In a
 headless VM `playOnce` is silently a no-op."
Object subclass: Tune [
    | abc |
    Tune class >> fromAbc: aString [ ^self new setAbc: aString ]
    setAbc: aString [ abc := aString. ^self ]
    playOnce [ "Play this tune once in the background." self primPlayTune: abc. ^self ]
    primPlayTune: s [ <primitive: 214> ^self ]
]
